   "verdict": "the 33x is ARITHMETIC, not a measurement: ratio == (nmax + baseline)/baseline at every nmax, and nmax is my free parameter (64). At nmax=256 it is 129x; at nmax=4 it is 3x. The magnitude was wrongly quoted as an empirical result.",
   "what_survives": "the SHAPE: first-position adoption imposes a CONSTANT per-miss overhead, so the penalty is unbounded as tasks get cheaper. Independently CONFIRMED on real problems where nothing is parameter-determined: on 26 QuixBugs, GlobalApply at cost_hint 1.0 (tried first) made gcd 1->15 evals (15x) and sieve 2->25 (12.5x), total +26% with zero extra solves."
  },
  "workload_C_cheap": {
   "arms": {
    "with_costed": {
     "evals": 12,
     "ratio": 1.0,
     "solved": 12
    },
    "with_first": {
     "evals": 12,
     "ratio": 1.0,
     "solved": 12
    },
    "without": {
     "evals": 12,
     "ratio": 1.0,
     "solved": 12
    }
   },
   "finding": "on CHEAP tasks (already in library, ~1 eval -- the regime of the existing 6-class matrix where disc_medE=1) the naive with_first adoption pays a fixed nmax probe on every miss. E-4s original B workload hid this because blind search there costs ~8600 evals, making a 64-eval probe 0.7% noise.",
   "n": 12,
   "recommendation": "ADOPT ITERATE, but COST-ORDERED (tried only after the cheap routes fail), which is exactly the projects existing cost_hint discipline. Naive first-position adoption is NOT unconditional."
  },
  "workload_D_adversarial": {
   "arms": {
    "with_costed": {
     "evals": 24,
     "ratio": 1.0,
     "solved": 12
    },
    "with_first": {
     "evals": 792,
     "ratio": 33.0,
     "solved": 12
    },
    "without": {
     "evals": 24,
     "ratio": 1.0,
     "solved": 12
    }
   },
   "design": "task is a CHEAP library hit that is NOT an iteration of the base, so ITERATE must exhaust nmax before failing -- the real S2-style collateral-damage case. My first cheap workload was badly built: the task WAS the base frame, so ITERATE succeeded at n=1 and no cost appeared. Corrected here.",
   "n": 12,
   "verdict": "with_first 33.0x (REGRESSION), with_costed 1.0x -> ADOPT COST-ORDERED ONLY"
  }
 },
